WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Vinegar owes its acidity to ethanoic
acid, also called acetic acid.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.780
It's a weak acid, with a pKa of 4.76.

3
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.320
Its molecule has four
hydrogen atoms, but only one,

4
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.260
the hydrogen on the oxygen, is acidic.

5
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.860
Now swap the other three
hydrogens for chlorine atoms.

6
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.040
The acidic hydrogen hasn't moved.

7
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.660
The pKa plunges to 0.66.

8
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.640
That's about 10,000 times stronger.

9
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:29.680
Why?

10
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:36.000
In this mission, you'll find out which hydrogens in
a molecule can leave as ions, and which never do.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.760
Then you'll line up related acids by pKa and
hunt for the pattern behind their strength.

12
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.840
Your mission: explain why three chlorines
make vinegar's acid so much stronger.